    <!-- Home shelf-tile chips, dark — see values/colors.xml. -->
    <!-- Downloads chip is the CORAL one (the brand tone, #F66A66). The
         old #5A2E2C was too dark/low-chroma and collapsed into brown;
         #74362A brightens + adds the orange lean (G above B) so it reads
         as a clear dark coral close to the brand. -->
    <color name="home_chip_downloads">#74362A</color>
    <color name="home_chip_downloads_on">#F6B6B3</color>
    <!-- Vault chip is the RASPBERRY one (its distinct "private corner"
         identity, set apart from the warm Downloads/Trackers chips). The
         old #552538 was so desaturated it read muddy/gold on a small dark
         chip; #62294A lifts the blue clearly above green so it reads pink
         even tiny and dark. -->
    <color name="home_chip_vault">#62294A</color>
    <color name="home_chip_vault_on">#F4A9C6</color>
    <!-- Trackers chip is the PEACH one (warm orange). #4A3A28 was the
         most desaturated of the trio and read as plain brown; #573B22
         warms it (bigger R-G gap) so it lands as peach and stays distinct
         from the coral Downloads and raspberry Vault. -->
    <color name="home_chip_trackers">#573B22</color>
    <color name="home_chip_trackers_on">#F3CBA3</color>
    <!-- Faded onPrimaryContainer (night #0F0000) for the download banner's
         progress track, so the bar reads in the text colour. ~30% alpha. -->

    <!-- Checked filter chip fill + label. Deliberately NOT
         md_theme_primaryContainer: in this palette the dark container (#F66A66)
         is within a hair of primary (#f0716c), so the checked chip landed at
         6.34:1 against the #131315 page — the brightest, most saturated thing
         on screen, competing with the artwork in the images mosaic. This is the
         same hue at a lower lightness: 5.28:1, a real step down, with the
         brand's own chroma (C* 55.2) so it doesn't go brown — brown is just
         low-chroma orange, and an earlier attempt at C* 47 read exactly that
         way.

         It stays a LIGHT FILL WITH A DARK LABEL, and that is the load-bearing
         part. Every filled control in this app is light-fill/dark-label — the
         primary button, the light-theme chip. A dark fill needs a light label,
         which makes the chip the only inverted control on the screen and reads
         as a different component family sitting right above the button. That
         was tried (#B23A3C + white, 3.15:1) and rejected on sight: calmer, but
         visibly a different species. Consistency is bought with some of the
         calm — if this ever needs to be quieter, lower the lightness only as
         far as the #460005 label still clears 4.5:1 (about L* 57), and never by
         inverting it. (The buy-credit segments are the ONE place that inversion
         is right, for the opposite reason — see
         @color/buy_segment_checked_container.) -->
    <color name="chip_checked_container">#DE615E</color>
    <color name="chip_checked_on_container">#460005</color>

    <!-- Checked buy-credit segment, dark — and note it INVERTS relative to the
         light theme (dark fill, light label). That is deliberate, and it is the
         one place the chip's never-invert rule above does NOT apply: a tonal
         segmented button is a DIFFERENT CONTROL CLASS from a filled button, and
         looking unlike the Continue CTA is the entire point. The chip's rule
         exists so a filled chip matches the filled button beside it; here the
         goal is the opposite.

         It also gives dark theme the real dark container tone this palette
         lacks. md_theme_secondaryContainer (what the segments used to take) is
         #FFA8A0 at L* 77 — a LIGHT fill under a DARK on-colour, the light-theme
         relationship copied into night, where M3 expects a dark container
         (tone ~30) under a light one. That put the checked segment at 10.04:1
         against the page while the CTA below it was 6.44:1: the mode selector
         out-shouting the button it feeds. (The pre-rotation apricot #FAB186 was
         10.34:1 — a lightness fault, not a hue one, predating the rotation.)
         Fixing the token itself would be correct and is NOT done here: it is a
         lightness inversion across ten consumer sites, any of which pairing it
         with hardcoded dark ink goes unreadable.

         #4A2120 is the fallback-thumbnail ground (MimeTypeThumbnail), already
         an app colour: ΔE 25.2 off the page, ΔE 55.6 off the CTA, label 10.62:1.
         The "−25%" badge inherits the label colour, so it stays legible in both
         states without a colour span. -->
    <color name="buy_segment_checked_container">#4A2120</color>
    <color name="buy_segment_checked_on_container">#FFDAD6</color>
    <!-- Progress-bar indicator — see values/colors.xml. Dark theme keeps the
         brand coral: on the #f0716c@20% track over a dark row it is 4.80:1. -->
    <color name="progress_indicator">#f0716c</color>
